diff --git a/docs/port/ATTACHED_EFFECTS.md b/docs/port/ATTACHED_EFFECTS.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..5784e4cd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/port/ATTACHED_EFFECTS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# Persistent effects and displayed poses
+
+R&C1 emits a ship's environment-mapped canopy as world-space effect quads,
+separately from its skeletal body. The frontend interpolates the body, its
+joints and camera between native ticks. Rendering the canopy at the current
+native tick while the body is between ticks makes the canopy run ahead of
+the ship. On a stationary ship the endpoints coincide, concealing the problem.
+
+The native environment-map callback now scopes its quads to their owner.
+`init_moby_instance` assigns each initialization a new host generation; the
+captured surface also records the class and an ordinal local to that owner.
+The native routines still run once, with unchanged arguments. These identities
+live in host memory and do not modify game progress, animation or physics.
+Particles, exhaust trails and unscoped effect quads retain their existing path.
+
+Only adjacent captured ticks with matching owner, generation, class and complete
+quad count supply interpolation endpoints. A reused slot, different topology,
+missing endpoint or singular transform starts from the current geometry.
+
+For each matched corner, the frontend removes the previous/current owner matrix
+from the respective world-space endpoint. It blends the resulting local posed
+coordinates and applies the exact owner matrix used to draw the skeletal mesh.
+This is the same matrix product as mesh rendering, including simultaneous
+object rotation/scale and joint motion. A skeleton discontinuity keeps the
+current local pose while following the body's displayed object transform.
+All inputs belong to the render snapshot; the worker never reads guest RAM.
+
+`effect_interpolation_test` covers a moving/scaling/turning owner with local
+joint motion, independent effects and owners, class/generation changes,
+changed or duplicate topology, missing endpoints and singular transforms.
+`effect_quad_test` retains coverage of native quad decoding and texture state.
+
+The regression was isolated with frozen Windows NTSC-U inputs: the same native
+flight frame showed a detached canopy with display interpolation and a closed
+canopy with the original frame rate. The fix preserves display interpolation
+and synchronizes the explicitly attached surface instead of disabling it.
diff --git a/docs/port/AUTHORED_CAMERA_CUTS.md b/docs/port/AUTHORED_CAMERA_CUTS.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c3f514ac
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/port/AUTHORED_CAMERA_CUTS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Authored cinematic camera cuts
+
+Ratchet & Clank's resident boarding and arrival timelines mark a camera cut in byte 12 of a 32-byte camera keyframe. The original update also forces the actor's odd-frame pose fraction to 1 at that keyframe. Those cuts are discontinuities even when the two camera positions and directions are close.
+
+The frontend previously inferred a cut only when the camera moved more than 8 game units or its forward-vector dot product fell below 0.7. Some authored cuts fall below both thresholds. Camera, object and joint interpolation could therefore create a picture between two distinct shots; attached effects and shadows also need to share that boundary.
+
+The internal game-state capture now includes an authored camera-cut bit. The RAC1 profile reads its own regional timeline layout through the runtime's existing executable and level relocation services. No native memory, clock, camera, actor, campaign or save value is changed. This is an internal profile/frontend change; the mod SDK remains 1.12.
+
+## Native provenance
+
+The resident update is PAL game/space.c update_resident_gameplay_state, or NTSC gameplay/update_resident_gameplay_state.c. It consumes camera byte 12, copies the camera position and rotation rows, and uses the flag when selecting the actor's native pose fraction. The bank parser is PAL func_00205220 or NTSC FUN_002049f0; its decompressed record supplies the camera offset, duration and actor count.
+
+The source-program addresses are independently specified:
+
+| Field | PAL canonical v2 | NTSC-U |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Shared ship record, executable source |0x13E130|0x13E030|
+| Playback record, executable source |0x18CC20|0x18CB20|
+| Native PAL timing selector, executable source |0x15EE80|0x15ED80|
+| Cinematic mode, level-zero source |0x15F6A8|0x15F5C4|
+
+The profile uses executable relocation for the first three fields and level-zero relocation for the mode. It never derives a regional address by adding a guessed offset. A viewer without runtime relocation callbacks simply has no authored timeline metadata.
+
+## Capture and rendering
+
+Only mode 6, resident ship states 0 or 8, and an active timeline may provide the flag. Before looking at the bank the reader requires a valid native 50/60 Hz selector, absolute time strictly below the signed 16-bit end, a coherent relative frame/bank, and a bounded actor count. It then checks the decompressed header's duration/count, derives the camera address from the record's own offset, checks it against the stored camera pointer and snapshot bounds, and rejects nonfinite camera fields. End cleanup returns before reading any retired bank pointer.
+
+The frontend excludes an authored cut from adjacent interpolation endpoints. A single helper discards the previous camera state, matrices, spheres, joint palettes, shadow capsules/state and attached effects together. The current captured shot remains intact. The next ordinary adjacent tick may interpolate from that exact shot normally.
+
+A monotonically advancing cut epoch travels with the owned scene snapshot. The render thread resets enhanced temporal history once on the next submitted picture with a new epoch. A display cap that omits the cut's own tick cannot lose that reset, and repeated intermediate pictures of one shot do not repeatedly reset accumulation. Render workers read no native RAM.
+
+## Verification boundary
+
+The cinematic_cut CPU test checks both regional profiles across 19 level identities and two relocation epochs, normal/flagged 50 Hz and 60 Hz banks, malformed pointers/topology/cursors, native cleanup with a retired invalid bank pointer, and a camera cut below both heuristic thresholds. It uses the actual captured scene type to verify that all previous endpoints are retired while the current scene survives.
+
+The focused suite also includes camera_target, frontend_geometry, effect_interpolation, frontend_pacing, transient_pacing and mod_transient. Windows NTSC-U and PAL runtime builds compile the same generic host support. Build and CPU evidence do not establish that a particular live transition is visually correct; that requires its own native capture.
+
+An optional offline diagnostic uses exactly the production reader on a private native memory capture:
+
+    cinematic_cut_test --ram capture.bin mode_address ship_address playback_address pal_mode_address
+
+Addresses are already relocated unsigned decimal values from that capture. The test requires an active authored cut, checks private in-memory copies with the flag cleared and with cleanup plus an invalid retired bank pointer, and runs the complete history endpoint test. Its JSON output distinguishes capture acceptance from the CPU endpoint test. It never changes the input file or starts a game. A raw game memory capture is local diagnostic data and must not be bundled with source, receipts or mod releases.
