diff --git a/docs/port/DESTINATION_HANDOFF.md b/docs/port/DESTINATION_HANDOFF.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..5ccba455
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/port/DESTINATION_HANDOFF.md
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# Experimental destination handoff (SDK 1.14)
+
+This isolated extension exposes the existing destination registry without a new
+menu. An installed destination and a campaign level are different routes, even
+when both use native program18. The installed manifest ID is a registry key;
+it does not authenticate a logical world's contents. A world's own marker/API
+can provide that additional identity. Registry order and ship-menu row numbers
+are never routing or save identifiers.
+
+The seven API pointers are appended after the eight SDK1.13 PCM pointers. No
+existing SDK field is moved. The destination descriptors and requests are
+size-tagged; all calls run on the game's thread. Outputs remain untouched on
+failure. Controls return `OPENRAC_HOOK_OK` on success. Tickets and handles are
+opaque, nonpersistent and generation-bound.
+
+## Sequence
+
+1. `destination_lookup(installed_id)` obtains an added destination, while
+   `destination_campaign(native_level)` obtains an explicit original route.
+   `destination_current` provides the logical origin and native load generation.
+2. After the native boarding has reached its ship-computer handoff (menu14,
+   ship state0, pause/map mode3), the mod calls `destination_begin_departure`. Its nonzero
+   nonce increases for each new departure by that mod in the same native load.
+   The request includes the current origin handle and both generations.
+3. On success, the mod invokes its existing native departure initializer. The
+   host consumes exactly this ticket and calls the normal shared SDK hook/game
+   chain, retaining the origin mount, authored dock pose, path and cameras.
+   Without a ticket, the official ship-menu implementation is unchanged.
+   A same-planet cancellation retains the ordinary native landing branch and
+   retires the ticket rather than waiting for a take-off that will not happen.
+4. The host surrounds the existing post-take-off `rac1.space.transition` hook.
+   It captures a handoff ID and initializes the pending program to the actual
+   logical origin. It does not change mounts or the currently loaded level.
+5. Inside that owner's transient scene, `destination_current` returns the
+   captured origin and nonzero handoff ID. `destination_prepare` binds the
+   target handle to that ID, owner, nonce, native generation and registry
+   generation. It changes no guest data or resource mount.
+6. Once `transient_run` has returned successfully and its closing callback,
+   audio and scene lease are fully released, `destination_commit` revalidates
+   every binding. It selects the official resource mount and stores the pending
+   native program consecutively on the game thread. There is no new boarding,
+   no second departure and no autonomous level-load request. The mod then calls
+   its existing native `original()` continuation exactly once.
+
+`cancel` is idempotent for a cancellation not subsequently consumed. An already
+consumed begin ticket or committed route returns `UNKNOWN`; cleanup may still
+forget its local handle. Scene error, cancellation, successful flight with no
+entry, abandonment and unload all retain the captured logical origin. In
+particular, a return to an added world on18 does not become campaign18.
+Wrong owners, repeated commit, stale tokens, missing registry entries or a
+changed native generation never select a different mount. A stale origin at
+native handoff is refused before continuing, rather than guessing another
+world with the same integer program.
+
+## Host implementation and limits
+
+`DestinationHandoff` is a bounded state machine with no per-request allocation.
+The registry retains the installed ID in the existing host map descriptor.
+`destination_registry.cpp` resolves it through that immutable session registry;
+there is no runtime registry-edit API. Each new process has a new handle domain.
+Future registry replacement must increment its generation before exposure.
+
+Selection reuses `openrac_game_select_destination`, including its resource-mount
+generation. The existing CD sector-patch cache and renderer both already
+invalidate on that generation, including a change between two worlds on18.
+The request never modifies inventories, visits, story coordinates, saves or the
+current native level. Original PAL destinations remain card-free as in0.10.
+This extension does not enable campaign-card support for added worlds.
+
+Boarding itself runs in mode6, but its native cleanup calls
+`PauseAllSounds(0)` (`func_00219C70`) before setting the menu to0xE. That call
+sets mode3. The departure guard therefore accepts only mode3, ship state0 and
+menu14 together; mode6 and the closing menu20 are not departure-ready. The
+PAL Batalia SDK1.14 smoke exposed this distinction at the actual API boundary.
+
+The R&C1 wrappers use existing canonical data and the version's existing
+relocation maps: pending program PAL0x0015F6E4 / NTSC-U0x0015F600; ship record,
+menu and level-mode fields already used by the original ship code. The owned
+branch calls the native initializer without rewriting its dock/path/camera
+inputs. No Gemlik overlay function is called from another loaded planet.
+
+CPU evidence covers the actual SDK DLL/loader, owner/nonce and lease boundaries,
+Home18 versus campaign18, error/cancel/fallback, missing/stale routes, official
+UI preservation, both regional wrapper continuations, and the pending-field
+store. These tests do not establish native visual, GPU, full-campaign or saved
+checkpoint compatibility. Those require the separately frozen world/mod builds.
diff --git a/docs/port/IO_TRACE.md b/docs/port/IO_TRACE.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..b6a822ea
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/port/IO_TRACE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Bounded native I/O diagnostics
+
+OPENRAC_TRACE_CD enables host records for disc reads, sync/error results and the stream-safe sound-library completion callback. With no diagnostic sink installed, the replacements preserve their existing behavior and the standalone CD reader retains its original stderr trace.
+
+Each program record contains a wall-clock timestamp, host frame, native frame when a checkpoint provides one, loaded overlay, native destination level, resident ship state, caller, sector/count/target, callback, argument/result, pending and error fields. These are command metadata; no disc payload or game-memory contents are logged. The sink inspects native state only on the owning game thread. Other threads report unavailable context instead of racing its clock or RAM.
+
+Repeated unchanged polling is sampled after its first four observations, at most once per second. Explicit fade-loop and bank-wait samples are limited the same way even when their remaining count changes. A run emits at most 8192 records. This tool deliberately adds diagnostic work and is not a performance benchmark.
+
+The direct disc replacement reads synchronously before returning; sceCdSync returns zero immediately and has no queue, timer or frame deadline. The stream-safe sound read uses that same synchronous path, then retains one completion pending bit. Its callback is dispatched on the next snd_FlushSoundCommands, after the native caller has marked the read active. The trace observes these existing operations without pumping, delaying or changing them.
+
+The PAL diagnostic recipe may separately instrument generated native C with temporary initialization/fade checkpoints. That script and its before/after source hashes live with the local QA receipts; those generated edits are not source changes and must be removed before an ordinary runtime build. The generated checkpoints revealed a source-overlay code-address collision: the transition archive read completed, but a mistyped scaler lookup passed 1446528 rather than 12 to FadeToBlack. This was a long native fade, not a CD sync wait.
+
+The cdvd_mount CPU test exercises the real replacement over synthetic sectors, checks that successful begin/end records retain command/result metadata, verifies that both sync modes still finish immediately, and confirms disabling the sink preserves reads without recording more events. It does not require a user disc or start a game.
diff --git a/docs/port/TRANSIENT_AUDIO.md b/docs/port/TRANSIENT_AUDIO.md
